---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const models = [
  { name: 'HTR-VT (augmented)', cer: '8.57%', note: 'Lowest reported CER' },
  { name: 'TrOCR (augmented)', cer: '8.69%', note: 'Stronger word- and line-level metrics' },
  { name: 'Kraken (augmented)', cer: '9.05%', note: 'CTC-based baseline' },
];
const augmentation = [
  { metric: 'Character Error Rate', without: '10.27%', with: '8.57%' },
  { metric: 'Word Error Rate', without: '44.11%', with: '36.91%' },
  { metric: 'Word F1', without: '0.6140', with: '0.6781' },
  { metric: 'Exact line accuracy', without: '11.33%', with: '16.53%' },
  { metric: 'Perfect lines', without: '205', with: '299' },
];
---

<Layout title="Bosančica AI — Tarik Agić">
  <main class="article">
    <a class="back" href="/work">← All projects</a>
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Computer Vision <span>·</span> Collaborative Research <span>·</span> 2025–2026</p>
      <h1>Bosančica AI</h1>
      <p class="dek">Handwritten text recognition for historical Bosančica manuscripts, combining document segmentation with a comparative evaluation of Kraken, TrOCR, and HTR-VT.</p>
      <div class="byline"><span>University of Sarajevo</span><span class="separator">·</span><span>HTR-VT research and implementation</span></div>
      <nav class="resources" aria-label="Project resources">
        <a href="https://bosancica.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Research interface ↗</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/Intellindust-AI-Lab/HTR-VT" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original HTR-VT architecture ↗</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="access-note">The research interface may require authorization. Project code, manuscript images, and the research manuscript are not linked here pending public-release approval.</p>
    </header>

    <article class="body">
      <section aria-labelledby="overview">
        <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>
        <p>Bosančica AI explores the digitization of historical manuscripts written in Bosančica, with the goal of converting scanned pages into editable and searchable text. The research combines line segmentation, expert-prepared reference transcriptions, and handwritten text recognition (HTR).</p>
        <p>The collaborative project studied three manuscript books, comprising approximately 375 pages, and compared three recognition approaches: Kraken, TrOCR, and HTR-VT. Beyond character accuracy, the evaluation examined word-level errors, exact-line recognition, model confidence, and inference speed. The system is a research prototype intended to support, rather than replace, expert review.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="pipeline">
        <h2 id="pipeline">Recognition pipeline</h2>
        <p>At the project level, manuscript pages are segmented into text lines before a recognition model produces character sequences. Kraken BLLA and manually corrected eScriptorium annotations supported segmentation; a geometric merging heuristic addressed fragmented baselines. The extracted lines were then evaluated using Kraken, TrOCR, and HTR-VT recognizers against expert reference text.</p>
        <div class="pipeline" aria-label="Manuscript recognition pipeline">
          <div><span class="step">01</span><strong>Manuscript pages</strong><small>Historical scans</small></div>
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <div><span class="step">02</span><strong>Line segmentation</strong><small>Detection and correction</small></div>
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <div><span class="step">03</span><strong>HTR recognition</strong><small>Kraken / TrOCR / HTR-VT</small></div>
          <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          <div><span class="step">04</span><strong>Text output</strong><small>Expert review</small></div>
        </div>
        <p>Segmentation, Kraken and TrOCR development, Unicode analysis, and the application interface were collaborative or teammate-owned workstreams. My primary responsibility was the HTR-VT recognizer and its evaluation.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="contribution">
        <h2 id="contribution">My contribution: HTR-VT</h2>
        <p>I led the complete <strong>HTR-VT workstream</strong>, adapting an existing published CNN–Vision Transformer–CTC architecture to the Bosančica corpus and its character vocabulary. The implementation uses a modified ResNet-18 feature extractor, four Vision Transformer encoder layers, and CTC-based sequence recognition.</p>
        <p>I trained controlled variants with and without image augmentation, using AdamW with Sharpness-Aware Minimization, span feature masking, exponential moving average weights, and validation-based checkpoint selection. The experiments used document-level training, validation, and test splits to reduce leakage between manuscript pages.</p>
        <p>I also conducted character-, word-, and line-level evaluation, paired statistical testing, error profiling, confidence calibration, and inference-speed benchmarking. The findings were documented for the project's final performance report and research manuscript. The HTR-VT architecture itself was developed by its original authors; my contribution was its adaptation, experimentation, and analysis.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="results">
        <h2 id="results">Experimental results</h2>
        <p>Image augmentation improved recognition performance on the same <strong>1,809-line HTR-VT test set</strong>. Character Error Rate decreased from 10.27% to 8.57% (a 16.6% relative reduction), while Word Error Rate decreased from 44.11% to 36.91%.</p>
        <div class="table-scroll"><table>
          <caption>Controlled HTR-VT augmentation experiment</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>No augmentation</th><th>Augmentation</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{augmentation.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.metric}</td><td class="numeric">{m.without}</td><td class="numeric">{m.with}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>The augmented model produced 299 perfectly recognized lines, compared with 205 without augmentation. A paired bootstrap analysis supported the observed CER improvement, with a reported 95% confidence interval for the CER difference of [−0.0188, −0.0153].</p>
        <div class="table-scroll"><table>
          <caption>Selected final recognition-model comparison (reported CER)</caption>
          <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>CER ↓</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{models.map((m) => <tr><td>{m.name}</td><td class="numeric">{m.cer}</td><td>{m.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Augmented HTR-VT achieved the <strong>lowest reported CER</strong> among the final configurations, while TrOCR performed better on word-level and complete-line metrics. Cross-model test-line counts differed slightly, so these figures should not be interpreted as a perfectly paired model comparison.</p>
        <p>In a batch-one inference benchmark on NVIDIA L40S hardware, HTR-VT processed approximately <strong>100 lines per second</strong>. These results characterize the reported experimental environment, not unrestricted real-world deployment performance.</p>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="team">
        <h2 id="team">Research team and status</h2>
        <p>The research team comprised <strong>Farah Piralić, Emir Baručija, Tarik Agić, and Emin Hadžiabdić</strong>, within the Data Science and Artificial Intelligence programme at the Faculty of Electrical Engineering, University of Sarajevo. The work spans document processing, multiple recognition architectures, and a research interface.</p>
        <p>The final performance report was completed in September 2026. A research manuscript is in preparation; its publication status and public availability should be confirmed before linking it. Manuscript imagery, reference text, and model artifacts are not reproduced here without confirmed publication permission.</p>
      </section>
    </article>
    <footer class="article-footer"><span>Python · PyTorch · Computer Vision · HTR · CTC</span><a href="/work">← Back to work</a></footer>
  </main>
</Layout>

